[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Requirements Structure in This Document

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Each requirement captured is augmented with *Criticality*, *Verification
Steps*, *Public Requirements References,* etc. e.g. see https://nmfta-repo.github.io/nmfta-vehicle_cybersecurity_requirements/?a=AA-010

   **UID**

shows a unique value assigned to the requirement for easy reference

   **CATEGORY**

groups like requirements together

   **CRITICALITY**

assigns a ‘priority’: a recommendation to the purchaser for each
requirement:

-  **High**: the working group advises that purchasers do not accept
   proposals that do not meet all ‘High’ criticality requirements

-  **Medium**: the working group advises that purchasers may accept
   proposals that do not meet ‘Medium’ criticality requirements when the
   failure is justifiable or mitigated by the OEM

-  **Low**: the working group advises that purchasers may accept
   proposals that do not meet ‘Low’ criticality requirements

..

   **CHILDREN**

Captures the applicable component categories; shows to which of the components of the system that this requirement applies by listing the child requirements that are specific to any of the classes of components.

   **PUB_REFS**

Captures the public requirements references / descriptions from external authoritative requirements as were known to the working group at the time of this draft. These references are included so that

-  Purchasers can easily refer to the referenced sections of the
   document for further clarification on what are acceptable norms when
   evaluating OEM responses to RFPs AND

-  OEMs can use the referenced sections of the documents for
   establishing common language and terms in the responses to RFPs to
   amortize the costs of developing detailed responses.

..

   **STATEMENT**

Shows the requirement as it applies to the components of a system.
The working group made every effort to make these
requirements shorter and more succinct than the authoritative external
references.

   **VERIFICATION**

shows the steps which can be executed by purchasers to confirm that a
given system satisfies this requirement. There are
several cases where the working group does not expect that purchasers
will perform their own verification. Where it is recommended that either
a third party be engaged to provide an analysis which can be used by the
purchasers to verify OEM claims, or that the OEM perform a
demonstration that the requirement is satisfied which can be observed
and confirmed by the purchaser. In such cases, rationale will be given.
Due to the costly nature of delegating to a third party or of preparing
a demonstration, this will only be recommended in cases where the
requirement has been listed as having high *Criticality*. Because of the
high *Criticality* of these requirements, it would be ideal to verify
them relying on both a third party and a demonstration; the
recommendation of the working group is that one or the other is
sufficient.

-  In the context of verification via reports from a third party it is
   acceptable to either, as a purchaser, contract the third party for
   testing or to verify documentation provided by a third party
   contracted by the OEM.

-  In the context of demonstration by the OEM, it is important that
   the purchaser ensure the demonstration covers the non-functional
   aspects of these requirements, (e.g. for secure boot it is not
   sufficient to demonstrate that valid images are bootable, but rather
   it is necessary to demonstrate that tampered images are not
   bootable.)

..

   **COMMENT**

Shows comments or notes from the working group.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Purpose, Architectural Scope & Classification Approach

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The **NMFTA Heavy-Duty Vehicle Cybersecurity Requirements (HD VCR)** specification establishes a comprehensive, structured set of cybersecurity requirements and verification procedures for Electronic Control Units (ECUs) and interconnected subsystems in commercial motor vehicles.

As modern commercial vehicles incorporate sophisticated electronic architectures, vehicle networks, and off-board connectivity, fleet managers and motor freight carriers bear increasing operational risk. The primary objectives of this specification are to:

1. **Empower Fleet Procurement**: Provide commercial fleets, motor carriers, and vehicle purchasers with actionable, prioritized cybersecurity criteria for evaluating vehicles, systems, and supplier proposals (RFPs).
2. **Standardize Supplier Expectations**: Define unambiguous, testable security controls for OEMs, Tier-1 suppliers, and aftermarket device manufacturers.
3. **Establish Verifiable Assurance**: Equip purchasers and independent testing laboratories with concrete verification procedures to validate that required controls are present and effective upon delivery.

Architectural Approach & ECU Classification:
=============================================
Commercial vehicle architectures partition electronic controllers into distinct device classes based on their network topology, external interfaces, gatewaying responsibilities, and overall risk profile:

- **Class 0 (Telematics)**: Systems possessing off-board Wide Area Network (WAN) connectivity (cellular, satellite, internet).
- **Class 1 (Multi-Segment with Wireless)**: Controllers connected to multiple internal buses with short-range wireless interfaces (Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, TPMS RF).
- **Class 2 (Vehicle Gateway)**: Dedicated routing, isolation, and message-filtering controllers (Central Gateway / CGW).
- **Class 3 (Multi-Segment with Untrusted Wired)**: Controllers bridging internal networks to untrusted physical connectors (OBD-II, RP1226, trailer bridges).
- **Class 4 (Multi-Segment Trusted)**: Multi-segment controllers where all attached buses are internal and trusted.
- **Class 5 (Single-Segment High Risk)**: Single-segment controllers whose disruption presents critical operational or safety impact (e.g., emissions/aftertreatment, retarders).
- **Class 6 (Single-Segment Medium Risk)**: Single-segment controllers of moderate operational risk (e.g., suspension, automated steering assists).
- **Class 7 (Single-Segment Low Risk)**: Single-segment controllers of low fleet risk (e.g., cab convenience, HVAC, door modules).

For physical vehicle controllers (Classes 1 through 7), requirements apply directly to the physical ECU hardware and its firmware. However, telematics systems (Class 0) represent a distributed system architecture requiring specialized decomposition, as described in the following section.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Telematics Systems in Commercial Fleets: Special Handling & Evaluation Guidance

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Special Handling for Class 0 Telematics Systems:
================================================
Unlike traditional on-vehicle ECUs (Classes 1 through 7) which are evaluated as individual physical modules, **Class 0 Telematics Systems** encompass a distributed ecosystem extending beyond the vehicle. A telematics solution typically spans four distinct architectural components:
1. An on-vehicle physical unit (*Vehicle Connection Component*),
2. A wireless pipe (*Connectivity or Communications Component*),
3. An off-board server infrastructure (*Cloud or Back-End Component*), and
4. A driver interface (*Mobile App or Driver Display Component*).

Because of this distributed nature, the telematics requirements derived from the NMFTA Telematics Security Requirements Matrix (TSRM) are handled specially:
- The specification partitions telematics controls across the four component boundaries.
- The supplier questionnaires generate four dedicated evaluation worksheets so that procuring fleets can obtain specific attestations from the respective engineering and operations teams.

Scope Guidance: Multiple Telematics Systems per Vehicle:
========================================================
Modern commercial motor vehicles frequently deploy **multiple distinct telematics systems** simultaneously. A single tractor or trailer may incorporate:

- An OEM factory telematics gateway / off-vehicle connectivity unit.
- An aftermarket Electronic Logging Device (ELD) or Fleet Management Information System (FMIS).
- Component-specific telematics devices (e.g., Engine, Transmission, or Braking/ABS telematics units).
- Camera, vision safety, and trailer tracking subsystems.

Because each deployed telematics system introduces network interfaces and potential attack vectors into the vehicle, **fleets and purchasers must evaluate ALL telematics systems installed on the vehicle**. 

When issuing supplier questionnaires or evaluating OEM compliance:
- Procuring fleets MUST ensure that questionnaire recipients complete responses for **ALL** telematics systems present on or integrated with the vehicle.
- A OEM or supplier providing multiple telematics subsystems must submit separate questionnaire responses for each individual system.
- An assessment of only one system (e.g., the primary ELD) does not confer cybersecurity assurance to other co-existing telematics systems (e.g., powertrain or trailer telematics).
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Requirements Structure and Options Handling (CONTAINS_OPTIONS)

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Note on Options for Requirements:
=================================
These specifications contain requirements where the working group cannot reasonably require a particular implementation option, only that one of the satisfactory ways to satisfy the requirement is implemented. Modelling these 'options' requirements as separate formal nodes is neither practical nor recommended: the sheer number of requirements captured for heavy vehicles means that the requirements must remain machine-readable and there is no machine-readable way to communicate options to implementors (for good reason).

The approach taken across all VCR requirements documents is to:
a) Include the options as notes to the implementers directly within the requirement statements or comments; and
b) Tag all requirements with `CONTAINS_OPTIONS` in their `TAGS` field so that the entire list of requirements containing notes to the implementers can be indexed for easy reference by them.
<<<

